, 0.02545166015625, -0.0163421630859375, 0.00791168212890625, 0.0014238357543945312, -0.003643035888671875, -0.01227569580078125, 0.007045745849609375, 0.0108642578125, -0.01480865478515625, -0.002864837646484375, -0.040130615234375, -0.007598876953125, -0.0258331298828125, -0.0004949569702148438, 0.01392364501953125, -0.01294708251953125, -0.0176544189453125, -0.0177764892578125, 0.00323486328125, -0.0131378173828125, 0.006839752197265625, 0.0125579833984375, 0.0545654296875, -0.035430908203125, -0.0176544189453125, -0.00205230712890625, 0.0102996826171875, -0.0232391357421875, -0.0019159317016601562, 0.01349639892578125, -0.001575469970703125, 0.00879669189453125, -0.041717529296875, -0.01003265380859375, -0.01497650146484375, -0.015350341796875, 0.03851318359375, -0.00787353515625, 0.036834716796875, -0.048431396484375, 0.0009627342224121094, -0.03387451171875, -0.006351470947265625, -0.0024814605712890625, -0.01151275634765625, -0.003932952880859375, -0.00319671630859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This brief work extends the permanental process model to include fixed effects. By placing a prior on the fixed effects coefficients, we show that, as the prior becomes increasingly non-informative, the intensity function of the permanental process can be derived via the representer theorem. This leads to a natural decomposition into a fixed effects term and a function residing in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS). We further demonstrate that the limiting equivalent kernel defines an RKHS whose squared norm exactly matches the limiting penalty. This framework provides a clear scientific interpretation of permanental process models and enables the incorporation of domain knowledge into the estimation procedure."}}